     1	# Decision 0009: the Rust driver pair, `rust-v1` and `rust-repository-v1`
     2	
     3	## Context
     4	
     5	Decision 0008 reserved six driver identifiers and bound each to the portable
     6	`manager-worker-v2` execution policy, receipt schema 3 for the local source mode
     7	and 4 for the external one, and the single artifact class
     8	`native-executable-v1`. Reservation is not admission: `rust-v1` and
     9	`rust-repository-v1` leave the reserved namespace only when this contract is
    10	accepted and `TASK-260728-251p01` moves them in the same change that mints the
    11	schema admitting them.
    12	
    13	Decision 0007 fixed one shared toolchain contract and left the `rust` registry
    14	entry reserved with an explicit obligation list — probe vectors, normalization,
    15	prerelease markers, root layout, primary-executable relpath, fingerprint
    16	algorithm, companions, platforms, baseline, `compatibility` granularity and
    17	initial set, and the metadata disposition table — to be completed on a qualified
    18	host rather than asserted.
    19	
    20	Rust is the hardest of the three reserved languages for one specific reason, and
    21	decision 0004 already recorded it as the ground for deferring Cargo: `cargo
    22	build` compiles and executes `build.rs`, and `rustc` loads and executes
    23	procedural macros inside the compiler. Both are package-selected code running
    24	during manager activity, which is the boundary the protocol exists to hold.
    25	Decision 0008 section 7 therefore does not ask how to accommodate them; it
    26	requires that every package-selected code-execution surface be rejected
    27	deterministically **before** the compile phase, and that a surface which cannot
    28	be is grounds to reject the driver.
    29	
    30	Rust adds a second problem the Go driver never had. `go build` links with the Go
    31	toolchain's own internal linker and starts nothing outside `GOROOT`. On macOS
    32	`rustc` resolves the linker as the name `cc` through `PATH` and locates the
    33	platform SDK by running `xcrun`. Both are measured below. Under decision 0008
    34	section 6 item 3 an executable outside the fingerprinted closure is not
    35	admissible, so a Rust driver either binds those components or rejects the
    36	platform — and macOS is the platform this release primarily targets.
    37	
    38	This decision resolves both problems with measurement rather than assertion, and
    39	fixes the complete `rust-v1` and `rust-repository-v1` contract: the trusted
    40	closure, the two argument vectors, the operation-private environment and
    41	manager-owned Cargo configuration, the exhaustive pre-compile rejection matrix,
    42	the Stage B disposition table and its acceptance layers, the artifact and
    43	identity model, and the platform matrix. The implementation-ready reference is
    44	[`docs/rust-build-drivers.md`](../docs/rust-build-drivers.md).
    45	
    46	### Evidence basis
    47	
    48	Every measured claim in this decision was produced on one host and is labelled
    49	as such. Nothing here is a platform claim; decision 0008 section 9 keeps both
    50	identifiers at an empty qualified-platform set until `TASK-260728-2bu2q6`.
    51	
    52	| | Value |
    53	|---|---|
    54	| Host | macOS 26.5, arm64 |
    55	| Rust | 1.91.0, `rustc 1.91.0 (f8297e351 2025-10-28)`, `cargo 1.91.0 (ea2d97820 2025-10-10)` |
    56	| Root | a directly resolved `rustup`-produced toolchain directory, never the `rustup` shim |
    57	| SDK | `MacOSX.sdk` from Xcode 26.5 |
    58	| Windows | no Rust toolchain present on the reachable Windows host (`TASK-260729-rhjxtx`, 19 cases `not_run`) |
    59	| Linux | not probed |
    60	
    61	`TASK-260729-rhjxtx` supplied the version, target, metadata-readability and
    62	selector measurements and is still review-pending; this decision re-measured
    63	each fact it relies on and records the re-measurement, so no unreviewed
    64	observation is load-bearing on its own.
    65	
    66	## Decision
    67	
    68	### 1. What the pair admits
    69	
    70	`rust-v1` compiles one Rust program from a vendored, dependency-closed build
    71	root inside the consuming skill snapshot. `rust-repository-v1` compiles one Rust
    72	program from a build root inside a locked external Git repository. The two share
    73	everything except source acquisition, audit subject, receipt schema, and marker
    74	state, exactly as `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` do.
    75	
    76	| | `rust-v1` | `rust-repository-v1` |
    77	|---|---|---|
    78	| Source mode | local snapshot | external locked repository |
    79	| Receipt schema | 3 | 4 |
    80	| Execution policy | `manager-worker-v2` | `manager-worker-v2` |
    81	| Source identity | `curator-build-source-v1` over the consuming snapshot | `curator-build-source-v1` over the external snapshot |
    82	| Command shape | `buildCommandV8` | `repositoryBuildCommandV2` + `skillBuildTargetV2` |
    83	| Project metadata | `Cargo.toml` at the build root | `Cargo.toml` at the selected build root |
    84	| Artifact class | `native-executable-v1` | `native-executable-v1` |
    85	
    86	`rust-repository-v1` reuses this decision's trusted closure, native target,
    87	argument vectors, environment, Cargo configuration, rejection matrix,
    88	disposition table, link policy, staging rules and no-execution rule without
    89	reinterpretation or widening. Where a rule below says "the driver", it means
    90	both identifiers.
    91	
    92	### 2. The trusted closure is two roots, and only one of them holds executables
    93	
    94	**Measured, macOS 26.5 arm64, Rust 1.91.0.** With `PATH` set to a directory
    95	containing twenty logging shims (`cc`, `clang`, `ld`, `xcrun`, `ar`, `dsymutil`,
    96	`sh`, `lld`, `ld64.lld`, `codesign` and ten more), each of which appends its own
    97	name and argv to a log file and exits 127:
    98	
    99	- a `cargo build` with no linker pinned and no SDK pinned resolved **`xcrun
   100	  --sdk macosx --show-sdk-path`** and then **`cc`** from `PATH`, and failed with
   101	  `error: linking with 'cc' failed: exit status: 127`;
   102	- the same build with `SDKROOT` set to an absolute SDK path and
   103	  `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` carrying
   104	  `-Clinker=<root>/lib/rustlib/<target>/bin/rust-lld` and
   105	  `-Clinker-flavor=ld64.lld` produced **zero** entries in the log, exited 0, and
   106	  produced a `Mach-O 64-bit executable arm64` that runs and whose only dynamic
   107	  dependency is `/usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib`;
   108	- the graph command `cargo metadata --format-version 1 --locked --offline`
   109	  produced **zero** entries in the log.
   110	
   111	The control is what makes the zero meaningful: the shims demonstrably fire when
   112	the pins are removed, in the same working directory, in the same run sequence.
   113	
   114	Two consequences are fixed here.
   115	
   116	**The process closure is exactly three executables, all inside the Rust
   117	distribution root.** `<root>/bin/cargo` is the driver's trusted launcher;
   118	`<root>/bin/rustc` and `<root>/lib/rustlib/<target>/bin/rust-lld` are the
   119	fingerprinted regular executables below it. No Apple `clang`, `ld`, `xcrun`,
   120	`ar` or `dsymutil` process participates. The `manager-worker-v2` graph is
   121	therefore satisfied by the primary root alone:
   122	
   123	```text
   124	manager parent
   125	  -> identity-verified manager-owned worker
   126	       -> fingerprinted <rust-root>/bin/cargo
   127	            -> fingerprinted <rust-root>/bin/rustc
   128	            -> fingerprinted <rust-root>/lib/rustlib/<target>/bin/rust-lld
   129	```
   130	
   131	**The platform SDK is bound as a second fingerprinted root that contributes no
   132	process.** It is data — headers, `.tbd` stubs and library stubs the linker
   133	reads. Decision 0008 section 6 item 3 names an SDK explicitly among the
   134	components a driver must bind or reject a platform over, so binding it is
   135	required even though it starts nothing.
   136	
   137	The `rust` registry entry therefore declares, per operating system, an ordered
   138	closed list of **link-support roles** the Rust distribution does not itself
   139	provide. On macOS that list is exactly one role, `platform-sdk`, and its value
   140	is data-only. A link-support root is resolved through the same two declaration
   141	channels decision 0007 section 3 fixes for a toolchain root — a root bundled
   142	with the manager distribution, or trusted operator configuration in
   143	manager-owned owner-protected state — and through nothing else. `PATH`, the
   144	inherited environment, `xcrun`, `xcode-select`, `DEVELOPER_DIR`, a package byte,
   145	a descriptor byte and a version-manager shim are all forbidden origins for it,
   146	with the same force and the same diagnostics as for the primary root. A missing
   147	or unusable link-support declaration is a Stage A failure before any source is
   148	acquired.
   149	
   150	This does **not** coin a new toolchain identifier. Decision 0007's closed set
   151	`{go, rust, swift, kotlin, jdk}` is untouched, the `rust` entry declares **no
   152	companion toolchain**, and `toolchain_identities` carries exactly one element
   153	for both drivers. What changes is the internal shape of the algorithm this
   154	decision owns: `curator-rust-toolchain-v1` fingerprints an ordered closure of
   155	roots rather than a single tree.
   156	
   157	`curator-rust-toolchain-v1` produces exactly:
   158	
   159	```json
   160	{
   161	  "algorithm": "curator-rust-toolchain-v1",
   162	  "rust_version": "release: 1.91.0",
   163	  "cargo_version": "cargo 1.91.0 (ea2d97820 2025-10-10)",
   164	  "launcher_relpath": "bin/cargo",
   165	  "rustc_relpath": "bin/rustc",
   166	  "linker_relpath": "lib/rustlib/aarch64-apple-darwin/bin/rust-lld",
   167	  "roots": [
   168	    {"role": "rust-distribution", "tree_sha256": "sha256:..."},
   169	    {"role": "platform-sdk", "tree_sha256": "sha256:..."}
   170	  ],
   171	  "closure_sha256": "sha256:..."
   172	}
   173	```
   174	
   175	`roles` is a closed manager-owned token set. No root *path* appears: toolchain
   176	location is not portable identity, exactly as decision 0007 section 3.2
   177	requires. Each per-root digest uses the same walk, ordering, record framing and
   178	link rules as `curator-go-toolchain-v1`, with its own domain prefix;
   179	`closure_sha256` is domain-separated over the ordered role and per-root digest
   180	pairs, so a two-root closure can never collide with a one-root closure that
   181	happens to hash the same bytes. `curator-go-toolchain-v1` is untouched, frozen
   182	and Go-only, and neither Rust driver reuses, extends or aliases it.
   183	
   184	**Measured fingerprint cost, same host.** The Rust distribution root is 657 MB
   185	across 167 regular files and hashes in 1.73 s wall clock; the SDK root is 261 MB
   186	across 32,345 regular files and 7,448 symlinks and hashes in 9.01 s. The cost is
   187	per operation and per root, it is dominated by the SDK's file count rather than
   188	its size, and it is stated rather than optimised away: memoising it across
   189	operations would weaken exactly the property decision 0007 says fingerprinting
   190	proves.
   191	
   192	### 3. Native target: representability is not admission
   193	
   194	**Measured, same host.** `rustc --print target-libdir --target
   195	x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` exits **0** and prints a path for a target whose
   196	standard library is absent from the tree; `cargo build --target` for that
   197	triple then fails only **after** `Compiling probe-positive v0.1.0`, that is
   198	after the compile phase has begun. An unknown triple is different:
   199	`--print target-libdir --target not-a-real-target` exits 1 before any
   200	compilation.
   201	
   202	The driver is native-only, so this matters as a gate rather than as a feature.
   203	The native target is `rustc --print host-tuple`, which on this host reports
   204	`aarch64-apple-darwin` and carries no platform-version component, so it is
   205	directly usable as a target identity without normalization. Admission is a
   206	manager-side check inside the fingerprinted tree: the directory
   207	`<rust-root>/lib/rustlib/<native-target>/lib` MUST exist and MUST contain a
   208	regular file matching `libstd-*.rlib`. A target whose standard library is absent
   209	fails Stage A with `build_toolchain_platform_unsupported`, before source
   210	acquisition and before any compiler child. `rustc --print target-list` and
   211	`--print target-libdir` are representability surfaces and MUST NOT be used as
   212	the admission test.
   213	
   214	Cross-compilation is forbidden. The compile command always passes `--target`
   215	with the resolved native tuple, so the target is manager-selected rather than
   216	inferred, and the artifact path is fully determined.
   217	
   218	### 4. Local source ownership: one build root, one package, one program
   219	
   220	`rust-v1` reuses the schema-6 and schema-7 `build_roots` model without change,
   218	### 4. Local source ownership: one build root, one package, one program
   219	
   220	`rust-v1` reuses the schema-6 and schema-7 `build_roots` model without change,
   221	and adds exactly the bindings decision 0008 section 4 requires of a local
   222	driver.
   223	
   224	- The build root MUST contain `Cargo.toml` directly, and that file MUST be the
   225	  nearest ancestor `Cargo.toml` of `source_dir`.
   226	- `source_dir` MUST equal its build root. A Rust package has no compilable
   227	  sub-unit that a directory path can name — a binary target is selected by name,
   228	  not by directory — so any other value would either be inert or would require a
   229	  package-controlled selector member the boundary forbids.
   230	- The build root MUST contain `Cargo.lock` directly and a `vendor` directory
   231	  directly. `vendor` MAY be empty; **measured** that an empty vendored directory
   232	  source resolves and reports metadata with exit 0.
   233	- The resolved graph MUST have exactly one workspace member, and it MUST be the
   234	  build-root package. **Measured** that a two-member workspace reports
   235	  `workspace_members` of length 2 and `resolve.root` of `null`, so both shapes
   236	  are decidable from the graph phase alone.
   237	- The build-root package MUST have exactly one target whose `kind` is exactly
   238	  `["bin"]`, and that target's name MUST match
   239	  `^[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,63}$`.
   240	
   241	That chain is the deterministic non-discovering mapping decision 0008 section 4
   242	demands: build root → its single package → its single `bin` target → one
   243	executable. The consuming manifest command key remains the sole naming
   244	authority; the target name is used only as the manager-derived
   245	`--bin` argument and to locate the produced file, and its grammar is checked
   246	before it reaches an argument vector.
   247	
   248	Two programs require two build roots. That duplicates a vendored tree, and it is
   249	the accepted cost of refusing a package-controlled target selector.
   250	
   251	### 5. External source ownership
   252	
   253	A `rust-repository-v1` command requires a `skill-build.json` **schema 2**
   254	descriptor. Against a schema-1 descriptor it fails
   255	`build_descriptor_driver_unsupported`, and against an unsupported descriptor
   256	version `build_descriptor_schema_unsupported`, with no fallback to another
   257	target, another driver, `go-repository-v1`, a script, a system command or a
   258	generic build facility.
   259	
   260	The descriptor target's `build_root` and `source_dir` carry their schema-1
   261	meaning. For this driver `source_dir` MUST equal `build_root`, by the same
   262	argument as section 4; `build_root` MAY be `.`, which the descriptor already
   263	admits and which does not affect the schema-6 prohibition on a local
   264	`build_root` of `.`. Every rule of section 4 about `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`,
   265	`vendor`, workspace membership and the single `bin` target applies unchanged to
   266	the selected external build root.
   267	
   268	The whole external snapshot remains the validation, identity and audit subject;
   269	only the selected build root is compiler-visible; no external repository byte is
   270	agent-facing or runtime-copied. Input MUST NOT come from the consuming skill,
   271	another external repository, a sibling or parent directory outside the selected
   272	build root, a host Cargo registry cache, a host `CARGO_HOME`, or the network.
   273	
   274	### 6. The fixed process graph and exactly two commands
   275	
   276	One `manager-worker-v2` session performs exactly one graph phase and exactly one
   277	compile phase. The driver uses the graph phase, so its session shape is the
   278	same "one graph command, one compile command" shape `manager-worker-v1` fixes
   279	for Go, under the v2 identity and the v2 process graph.
   280	
   281	With the canonical `source_dir` as the working directory, the manager MUST use
   282	exactly these two argument vectors and MUST NOT alter, extend, reorder or repeat
   283	them:
   284	
   285	```text
   286	cargo metadata --format-version 1 --locked --offline --color never --quiet
   287	cargo build --locked --offline --color never --quiet --release --target <native-tuple> --bin <bin-target-name>
   288	```
   289	
   290	`<native-tuple>` is the Stage A resolved host tuple and `<bin-target-name>` is
   291	the validated single `bin` target name from the graph phase. Both are
   292	manager-derived from validated manager-owned data; neither is copied from a
   293	manifest, a descriptor, or an unvalidated package string.
   294	
   295	Three package-independent probe vectors run once per operation from the manager
   296	parent during Stage A, from a manager-owned empty working directory:
   297	
   298	```text
   299	rustc -vV
   300	rustc --print host-tuple
   301	cargo --version
   302	```
   303	
   304	`--locked` is load-bearing and not a convenience. **Measured** that `cargo
   305	metadata` without it *writes* `Cargo.lock` into the source tree — a write to the
   306	frozen snapshot — and that with `--locked` and no lock file present it instead
   307	exits **101** with `error: the lock file ... needs to be updated but --locked
   308	was passed to prevent this` and writes nothing.
   309	
   310	The produced file is `<CARGO_TARGET_DIR>/<native-tuple>/release/<bin-target-name>`
   311	on Unix and `...\release\<bin-target-name>.exe` on Windows. `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`
   312	is an operation-private manager staging root, so the artifact is produced inside
   313	manager staging, is hashed there, and is published as `bin/<command>` or
   314	`bin/<command>.exe` derived solely from the consuming manifest command key.
   315	
   316	### 7. The operation-private environment and the manager-owned Cargo configuration
   317	
   318	The environment starts empty except for indispensable operating-system process
   319	variables. It sets exactly the variables the reference document enumerates:
   320	private `HOME`, `TMPDIR`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, `XDG_CACHE_HOME`, `CARGO_HOME` and
   321	`CARGO_TARGET_DIR` (and on Windows private `APPDATA`, `LOCALAPPDATA`,
   322	`USERPROFILE`, `TEMP` and `TMP`); `PATH` naming a manager-owned empty directory;
   323	`LC_ALL=C` and `LANG=C`; absolute `RUSTC` and `RUSTDOC` inside the fingerprinted
   324	root; empty `RUSTC_WRAPPER` and `RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER`;
   325	`CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` carrying exactly the fixed linker pins;
   326	`CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0`; `CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true`, `CARGO_NET_RETRY=0`,
   327	`CARGO_NET_GIT_FETCH_WITH_CLI=false`; and on macOS `SDKROOT` naming the resolved
   328	`platform-sdk` root. No Rust, Cargo, `rustup`, compiler, linker, SDK or
   329	executable-search variable is inherited, and `RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP` MUST be absent
   330	because it turns a release toolchain into one that accepts `-Z` flags.
   331	
   332	Two of those settings are answers to measured attacks rather than hygiene.
   333	
   334	**`RUSTC` must be absolute.** `TASK-260729-rhjxtx` measured that cargo resolves
   335	`rustc` by name from `PATH` and fails with `could not execute process 'rustc
   336	-vV' (never executed)` under a minimal `PATH`; without an explicit `RUSTC` the
   337	second node of the process graph would be chosen by `PATH` order rather than by
   338	the fingerprinted closure. This decision re-measured the working direction: with
   339	absolute `RUSTC` and an empty `PATH`, no `PATH` lookup occurs at all.
   340	
   341	**A package `.cargo/config.toml` is a live process-injection surface.**
   342	**Measured**: a `.cargo/config.toml` at the build root carrying `[build]
   343	rustc-wrapper = "/tmp/probe-wrapper.sh"` caused that script to be executed three
   344	times during `cargo build`, while the manager's `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` still
   345	took precedence over the same file's `rustflags`. **Measured**: a
   346	`.cargo/config.toml` in an *ancestor* directory above the build root is
   347	discovered and applied the same way. **Measured**: setting `RUSTC_WRAPPER` to
   348	the empty string in the manager environment neutralises both, and a config
   349	`[env] RUSTC_WRAPPER = { value = "...", force = true }` does **not** override it.
   350	
   351	Both defences are kept, and neither is trusted alone. The environment
   352	neutralises the settings that have an environment counterpart; the rejection
   353	matrix of section 8 refuses the file outright, because `[source]` replacement,
   354	`[registries]`, `[patch]` and `[http]` have no environment counterpart and a
   355	package config file takes precedence over the manager's own `$CARGO_HOME`
   356	config. In addition, the manager MUST guarantee that no `.cargo` directory
   357	exists in any ancestor of the compile working directory; that is a manager
   358	staging obligation, not a package check, and it fails
   359	`build_execution_control_unavailable` when it cannot be met.
   360	
   361	The manager writes `$CARGO_HOME/config.toml` itself, with a fixed grammar and
   362	exactly four tables — `[source.crates-io]` replacing with `curator-vendor`,
   363	`[source.curator-vendor]` naming the absolute path of `<build_root>/vendor`,
   364	`[net] offline = true`, and `[term]` fixing quiet and colour. The only
   365	package-derived component is the driver-fixed relative path `vendor`; nothing a
   366	package writes reaches that file.
   367	
   368	### 8. The exhaustive pre-compile rejection matrix
   369	
   370	The matrix is computed from the validated snapshot and the graph phase, before
   371	the compile phase, and it is total: every surface below has exactly one verdict,
   372	and a surface that cannot be decided there is grounds to reject the driver
   373	rather than to add a runtime allowance.
   374	
   375	Three properties hold across the whole matrix.
   376	
   377	**The graph phase executes no package code.** **Measured**: a build root whose
   378	path dependency declares `build = "build.rs"` and whose `build.rs` writes a
   379	marker file, together with a second path dependency whose library is
   380	`proc-macro = true` and whose macro writes a second marker file, produced
   381	**neither** marker under `cargo metadata --format-version 1 --offline`, which
   382	exited 0. This is the Rust analogue of the Swift `dump-package` finding, with
   383	the opposite result: Rust's metadata query is bounded and does not compile or
   384	run the package.
   385	
   386	**The surfaces that matter are visible in that output.** **Measured**, the same
   387	run reported `kind: ["custom-build"]` for the build script target, `kind:
   388	["proc-macro"]` and `crate_types: ["proc-macro"]` for the macro crate,
   389	`links: "probelib"` for the package declaring a native library, and `source`
   390	per package.
   391	
   392	**The matrix is host-independent.** The graph command is run without
   393	`--filter-platform`, so `packages[]` is the union over every platform and every
   394	feature rather than the resolution for this host. That is a deliberate
   395	over-approximation: it is a superset of what the compile phase builds, so it can
   396	never miss an admitted-and-then-executed surface, and the same snapshot produces
   397	the same verdict on every host. Its cost is that a dependency that would only be
   398	built on another platform, or only under a feature this build does not enable,
   399	still rejects the command. That cost is accepted and stated rather than traded
   400	for a host-dependent verdict.
   401	
   402	| Surface | Verdict | Decided by |
   403	|---|---|---|
   404	| build script (`build.rs`, any `build` key, any `custom-build` target in any package) | reject | graph phase |
   405	| procedural macro (`proc-macro` target kind or crate type in any package) | reject | graph phase |
   406	| compiler plugin, codegen backend, `-Z` flag, `cargo-features` manifest key, `RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP` | reject | snapshot bytes and fixed environment |
   407	| `rustc-wrapper`, `rustc-workspace-wrapper`, any `.cargo` directory in the build root subtree | reject | snapshot bytes, plus empty-valued environment |
   408	| `links` key on any package | reject | graph phase |
   409	| dependency source other than a build-root path package or the vendored crates.io registry — git, alternate registry, local-registry, unknown | reject | graph phase |
   410	| `[patch]`, `[replace]`, `[workspace]` table in the build-root manifest | reject | snapshot bytes |
   411	| more than one workspace member, or a virtual manifest | reject | graph phase |
   412	| zero or more than one `bin` target in the build-root package | reject | graph phase |
   413	| `crate_types` outside `{bin, lib, rlib}` — `dylib`, `cdylib`, `staticlib`, `proc-macro` | reject | graph phase |
   414	| a package file or target source outside the build root, or a registry package outside `<build_root>/vendor` | reject | graph phase |
   415	| native object, archive, shared library or foreign source file in the compiler-visible tree, by closed extension list | reject | snapshot bytes |
   416	| network and registry access, index update, git fetch | reject | `--offline`, `--locked`, manager-written config, empty `PATH` |
   417	| package-selected linker, linker flavour, link argument, library search path | reject | fixed `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS`, no config file, no build script |
   418	| package-selected toolchain path, root, channel, mirror, installer, version manager | reject | decision 0007 resolution, and `rust-toolchain*` classified rather than honoured |
   419	| cross-compilation, non-native `--target` | reject | fixed compile vector |
   420	| Cargo features and `[profile]` tables | admit | see below |
   421	| `#[link]` attributes and `extern` blocks in package source | admit, bounded | see section 13 |
   422	| `include!`, `include_str!`, `include_bytes!` | admit, bounded | see section 13 |
   423	
   424	Cargo features are admitted because they select which package source compiles,
   425	which is the same class of choice as a Go build constraint, and because the
   426	manifest command object cannot express one — the build always uses the root
   427	package's default feature resolution. `[profile]` tables are admitted for the
   428	same reason and one measurement: on a release toolchain, per-profile `rustflags`
   429	are gated behind `-Z profile-rustflags`, and the driver rejects both `-Z` flags
   430	and the `cargo-features` opt-in that would unlock them, so a profile can select
   431	codegen tuning and cannot inject a flag or a process. A `[profile]` table that
   432	enables link-time optimisation stays inside `rustc` and its bundled LLVM; it
   433	starts no additional process.
   434	
   435	The shared semantic class for a rejection in this matrix is
   436	`build_package_code_execution_forbidden`; the reference document names the
   437	per-surface diagnostic beneath it for each row.
   438	
