   459	with the rule "above resolved → mismatch". That is right for the host relation
   460	and incomplete as a classifier, because the field has three host-independent
   461	acceptance layers before the host relation is reachable at all.
   462	
   463	**Measured**, `cargo metadata --no-deps --format-version 1 --offline`, one value
   464	per fixture, exit code and first diagnostic line:
   465	
   466	| Value | Exit | Layer that rejected | First line |
   467	|---|---|---|---|
   468	| `"1.85"` | 0 | — | accepted, `rust_version` `1.85` |
   469	| `"1.85.0"` | 0 | — | accepted, `rust_version` `1.85.0` |
   470	| `1.85` (TOML float) | 101 | document | `error: invalid type: floating point '1.85', expected a semver or workspace` |
   471	| `"1.85.0-beta"` | 101 | grammar | `error: unexpected prerelease field, expected a version like "1.32"` |
   472	| `"not-a-version"` | 101 | grammar | `error: unexpected prerelease field, expected a version like "1.32"` |
   473	| `"1.85.0+build"` | 101 | grammar | `error: unexpected build field, expected a version like "1.32"` |
   474	| `"1.85.0.1"` | 101 | grammar | `error: expected a version like "1.32"` |
   475	| `"stable"` | 101 | grammar | `error: expected a version like "1.32"` |
   476	| `""` | 101 | grammar | `error: expected a version like "1.32"` |
   477	| `"1"` | 101 | edition floor | `error: failed to parse manifest at '<path>'`, caused by `rust-version 1 is older than first version (1.56.0) required by the specified edition (2021)` |
   478	
   479	So Rust's structure is **three host-independent layers plus one host gate**,
   480	where Go has two plus one:
   481	
   482	| Layer | What it decides | Host input |
   483	|---|---|---|
   484	| document | the TOML document parses and the value has an admissible type | none |
   485	| grammar | cargo's `rust-version` grammar can represent the value | none |
   486	| edition floor | the value is not below the floor the manifest's own `edition` requires | none |
   487	| host gate | the resolved toolchain satisfies the value | the resolved toolchain |
   488	
   489	The three layers are pairwise independent in the same sense decision 0007
   490	established for Go, and the independence is measured rather than argued: the
   491	document layer accepts `"not-a-version"`, which the grammar layer rejects; the
   492	grammar layer accepts `"1"`, which the edition floor rejects; and the edition
   493	floor would accept `1.85`, which the document layer rejects as a float. No layer
   494	contains another, so a classifier pinned to any one of them admits values the
   495	others refuse.
   496	
   497	The host gate is separate and is **excluded from the layer measurement**, for
   498	exactly the reason decision 0007 gives: a gate that depends on the runner cannot
   499	be part of a value's grammar. **Measured**, `cargo build --offline` with
   500	`rust-version = "1.99"` on a 1.91.0 host exits 101 with `error: rustc 1.91.0 is
   501	not supported by the following package:` and `probe-future@0.1.0 requires rustc
   502	1.99`, and `build_started_compilation` is false — cargo applies the gate before
   503	compiling. Curator does not wait for it: the value is compared against the
   504	resolved toolchain identity in Stage B, so the rejection happens before cache
   505	lookup and before any compiler child.
   506	
   507	Rust is in one respect better placed than Go. The isolated representability
   508	measurement needs no harness surgery: `cargo metadata --no-deps` reports
   509	`rust_version` for a value above the host (**measured**, `"1.99"` on a 1.91.0
   510	host, exit 0) and so structurally cannot be applying the host gate. The
   511	corroborating command is `cargo build --offline`, whose outcome is classified
   512	into `accepted`, `rejected-document`, `rejected-grammar`, `rejected-edition`,
   513	`host-gate` and `unknown`, never into pass and fail, and which is required to
   514	agree with the isolated measurement on the three layers.
   515	
   516	The recognised outcome set is closed and whole-line exact. Every rejection above
   517	renders a caret block whose third line is `5 | rust-version = <literal>` — the
   518	literal under test at a line number the probe fixture fixes — so a recognised
   519	outcome is the pair of one exactly predicted first line and one exactly
   520	predicted source line, and the upstream constants it embeds (`"1.32"`, the
   521	`1.56.0` edition floor, the edition token) are probe fixed constants. Anything
   522	else is **unknown**, yields no verdict and fails the probe. A lead with an
   523	unconstrained tail, and a substring found anywhere in the output, are families
   524	rather than outcomes and MUST NOT be recognised. Closure is measured, not
   525	asserted: the probe feeds the classifier unrelated real command failures, every
   526	measured outcome cross-fed under the wrong value, and measured diagnostics
   527	extended the way a later release would extend them, and requires each to produce
   528	no verdict, reporting which laundering direction each fabrication belongs to.
   529	
   530	The confirmed and corrected `rust` disposition table is:
   531	
   532	| Source | Field | Disposition |
   533	|---|---|---|
   534	| `Cargo.toml` | `package.rust-version` | `classified` — three layers plus the host relation, per the reference document |
   535	| `Cargo.toml` | `workspace.package.rust-version` | not reachable: a workspace is rejected by section 8, so this row is unreachable rather than admitted |
   536	| `rust-toolchain.toml` | `toolchain.path` | `forbidden` |
   537	| `rust-toolchain.toml` | `toolchain.channel` | `compared`, by decision 0007's channel table |
   538	| `rust-toolchain.toml` | `toolchain.components`, `targets`, `profile` | `ignored` |
   539	| `rust-toolchain` (legacy one-line form) | the bare channel string | `compared`, same classifier as `toolchain.channel` |
   540	
   541	`rust-version` moves from `compared` to `classified` for the same structural
   542	reason decision 0007 classified the two `go.mod` directives: one field whose
   543	value space spans more than one disposition needs an ordered exhaustive value
   544	classifier, not a single comparison rule. It gains no `forbidden` class, because
   545	its value space is a version and nothing else. This is a narrowing of what
   546	Curator accepts silently, not a widening, and it introduces no selector.
   547	
   548	Both `rust-toolchain` files are evaluated in Unicode-scalar lexical order of
   549	relative source path, so `rust-toolchain` precedes `rust-toolchain.toml`, and
   550	within each file `forbidden` precedes `compared`, so a file carrying both `path`
   551	and `nightly` is deterministically a package-influence rejection.
   552	
   553	### 10. The `rust` registry entry
   554	
   555	| Field | Value |
   556	|---|---|
   557	| `toolchain_id` | `rust` |
   558	| `primary_relpath` | `bin/cargo`; `bin/cargo.exe` on Windows |
   559	| `probe` | `rustc -vV`, `rustc --print host-tuple`, `cargo --version`, from a manager-owned empty working directory under the operation-private environment |
   560	| `normalization` | the single line of `rustc -vV` **stdout** matching `^release: (0\|[1-9][0-9]*)\.(0\|[1-9][0-9]*)\.(0\|[1-9][0-9]*)(-[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?$`; a non-empty fourth group is a prerelease; `cargo --version` stdout must normalize to the same triple or the version is undetermined |
   561	| `fingerprint_algorithm` | `curator-rust-toolchain-v1`, section 2 |
   562	| `baseline` | `{"kind":"at_least","min":"1.91.0"}` |
   563	| `compatibility` | families `{(1, 91)}`; family granularity `(major, minor)` |
   564	| `platforms` | `(macos, arm64)` only |
   565	| `companions` | none |
   566	| `link_support_roles` | `macos`: exactly `[platform-sdk]`, data-only |
   567	| `metadata_sources` | `Cargo.toml` `package.rust-version`; `rust-toolchain.toml`; `rust-toolchain` |
   568	
   569	`rustc --version` is deliberately not the version probe: it embeds a commit hash
   570	on the same line, so the verbose form is the narrower surface. The `host:` line
   571	of `rustc -vV` carries the same value as `--print host-tuple` on this host and is
   572	deliberately not the target probe, for the same reason — the dedicated print is
   573	narrower.
   574	
   575	The baseline and the compatibility set are both `1.91` because 1.91.0 is the
   576	only release this contract was measured against. Lowering the baseline requires
   577	measuring the older release; adding a family requires testing it against the
   578	driver's conformance vectors. Neither may be derived from version ordering, and
   579	no package byte can reach either.
   580	
   581	`platforms` holds one pair, and that is the honest consequence of the evidence
   582	rather than a scoping choice. `(macos, amd64)` is a qualification obligation
   583	with a stated acceptance test, not a claim: the Rust root ships an
   584	`x86_64-apple-darwin` standard library, but no x86_64 macOS host was available,
   585	so the pair is absent until a probe run on one exists. On a host whose pair is
   586	not in `platforms`, Stage A fails `build_toolchain_platform_unsupported` from
   587	the pre-resolution half of the check, on registry data alone.
   588	
   589	### 11. Artifact, cache, receipt, marker and claim identity
   590	
   591	The artifact class is `native-executable-v1` and nothing else. **Measured** on
   592	this host that the pipeline produces exactly one Mach-O arm64 executable whose
   593	sole dynamic dependency is `/usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib` — a base-installation
   594	library — and that it is `adhoc, linker-signed`. That signature is applied by
   595	`rust-lld` as part of linking, which is exactly the case decision 0008 section 3
   596	names as compiler output rather than a manager signing step: it is produced by
   597	the driver's fixed argument vector, selects no signing identity, credential or
   598	notarization, and reaches no network. The driver performs no manager
   599	post-build signing, and a platform policy requiring a locally signed binary must
   600	wait for the separately reviewed signer profile.
   601	
   602	Every other file cargo leaves in the target directory — rlibs, `.rmeta`,
   603	dependency info, incremental state, split debug information — is a compiler
   604	by-product. It stays in operation-private staging, is discarded with it, and
   605	never enters cache identity, the receipt, the marker, the shim relationship, or
   606	publication. The manager MUST NOT execute the artifact for validation, version
   607	discovery, smoke testing, post-processing, receipt generation, rollback or any
   608	other reason.
   609	
   610	The canonical build input binds, in addition to the members decision 0008
   611	section 8 requires of every new driver, the complete
   612	`curator-rust-toolchain-v1` identity of section 2 — including both root digests
   613	and the closure digest — the native tuple, the validated `bin` target name, and
   614	this closed policy object:
   615	
   616	```json
   617	{
   618	  "dependency_mode": "vendor-directory",
   619	  "network": "none",
   620	  "workspace": false,
   621	  "build_scripts": false,
   622	  "proc_macros": false,
   623	  "plugins": false,
   624	  "features": "manifest-default",
   625	  "target_mode": "native",
   626	  "profile": "release",
   627	  "linker": "toolchain-rust-lld",
   628	  "link_mode": "internal",
   629	  "native_inputs": false,
   630	  "package_config": "rejected",
   631	  "compiler_directives": "reject-nonstandard-native-inputs-v1",
   632	  "incremental": false,
   633	  "execution_policy": "manager-worker-v2"
   634	}
   635	```
   636	
   637	`execution_policy` is the `const` `manager-worker-v2`, so a Rust entry can never
   638	alias a Go entry, and `driver` differs besides. `network: "none"` denotes the
   639	fixed offline Cargo configuration, `--offline`, `--locked`, the manager-written
   640	`$CARGO_HOME` config and the empty `PATH`; it is not a claim of kernel-enforced
   641	network denial, which remains the deferred `total-network-denial` guarantee.
   642	
   643	Receipt schema 3 carries the local mode and schema 4 the external mode, each a
   644	strict `oneOf` discriminated by the `driver` `const`, each carrying this policy
   645	object, this toolchain identity and this native target. Marker schema 4 records
   646	the driver, `receipt_schema_version` and `execution_policy` per build entry, and
   647	a reader rejects a `rust-v1` entry claiming `manager-worker-v1` rather than
   648	inferring the policy from the driver name. Conformance claim schema 4 asserts
   649	`rust-v1` and `rust-repository-v1` with `execution_policy` `manager-worker-v2`
   650	bound by the assertion's own `driver` `const`, and only if this contract is
   651	accepted and `TASK-260728-251p01` moves both identifiers in the same change that
   652	mints the schema.
   653	
   654	The effective toolchain requirement and the `compatibility` set stay gates
   655	rather than build inputs, exactly as decision 0007 fixes them.
