   700	### 13. Two residual exposures, stated rather than closed
   701	
   702	Decision 0008's security section requires each driver contract to state its own
   703	compiler-input exposure honestly and to refrain from relying on containment this
   704	protocol does not provide. Two Rust surfaces are admitted with bounds rather
   705	than rejected, and both are named here so that no reader, receipt, marker or
   706	claim can imply otherwise.
   707	
   708	**Compile-time file inclusion.** `include!`, `include_str!` and `include_bytes!`
   709	resolve a path relative to the including file and can name a path outside the
   710	build root. They are **reads**, not code execution, so decision 0008 section 7
   711	does not require their rejection; but a read that lands in the produced binary
   712	is an exfiltration surface, and the portable policy does not contain the
   713	compiler's filesystem access. No deterministic pre-compile rejection exists that
   714	is sound in both directions: the macro name is a token, not a byte pattern —
   715	`include ! ( "x" )` and `#[cfg_attr(unix, ...)]` forms defeat a byte scan, while
   716	a scan for the substring `include` rejects ordinary comments and identifiers.
   717	The surface is therefore admitted, bounded only by the operation-private
   718	environment and the frozen snapshot, and recorded as an input to
   719	`STORY-260728-327soo`: none of the six deferred hardened guarantees currently
   720	covers compile-time filesystem reads, so closing it needs a seventh.
   721	
   722	**Foreign function declarations against base-installation libraries.** Package
   723	source may declare `extern` blocks and `#[link]` attributes. The package cannot
   724	supply a library to link — native files in the tree are rejected, `links` is
   725	rejected, build scripts are rejected, and there is no admitted path that adds a
   726	library search path — so `#[link]` can only name a library the pinned link
   727	environment already resolves, which is a base-installation library. Decision
   728	0008 section 3 already requires the artifact to depend on exactly those, so this
   729	is inside the artifact class rather than an escape from it. What it is not is a
   730	guarantee that the produced program is safe; the artifact remains untrusted
   731	package output that the manager never executes.
   732	
   733	Both statements are about the produced program and about compile-time reads.
   734	Neither weakens the process-graph, network, write or resource properties of
   735	section 2 and section 7.
