   759	### 15. Enforcement
   760	
   761	The boundary gate of decision 0008 section 11 needs no new mechanism for this
   762	contract: `rust-v1` and `rust-repository-v1` are already two of the six reserved
   763	identifiers it holds out of every surface file, and the exact member-set table
   764	already covers the shapes they will occupy. Three additions belong to
   765	`TASK-260728-251p01` at admission time and are named here so the gate is
   766	extended rather than weakened:
   767	
   768	1. the two identifiers move from the reserved namespace to the admitted set in
   769	   the same change that mints receipt schemas 3 and 4, and the gate's driver
   770	   `const` sets move with them;
   771	2. the policy object of section 11 joins the exact member-set table, closed and
   772	   `additionalProperties: false`, with `execution_policy` pinned to the
   773	   `manager-worker-v2` `const`; and
   774	3. the `curator-rust-toolchain-v1` identity joins the table as an object schema
   775	   with its closed member set, its `roles` token set closed by `const`, and no
   776	   member naming a filesystem path.
   777	
   778	## Stable failure classes
   779	
   780	These are architecture-level semantic classes and MUST remain distinguishable
   781	from each other, from a cache hit, from an audit success, from source
   782	unavailability and from a generic fallback. The reference document maps each to
   783	its exact trigger and stage.
   784	
   785	- `build_package_code_execution_forbidden`, the shared class of section 8;
   786	- `build_descriptor_driver_unsupported` and
   787	  `build_descriptor_schema_unsupported`, unchanged from decision 0008;
   788	- `build_artifact_class_unsupported`, for a platform that cannot produce a
   789	  single self-contained executable; and
   790	- the twelve `build_toolchain_*` codes of decision 0007, unchanged, with
   791	  `build_toolchain_platform_unsupported` carrying both the unsupported host pair
   792	  and the absent-standard-library case of section 3, and
   793	  `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch` carrying the `rust-version` classifier of
   794	  section 9.
   795	
   796	## Rejected alternatives
   797	
   798	- **Reject Cargo entirely and drive `rustc` directly, as decision 0004
   799	  contemplated.** Rejected: for anything with a dependency it means
   800	  re-implementing resolution and ordering inside the manager and issuing one
   801	  `rustc` invocation per crate, which the `manager-worker-v2` session shape —
   802	  exactly one compile command — does not admit. The narrower fix was taken
   803	  instead: keep `cargo` as the single launcher and remove every surface through
   804	  which a package can make it start something else.
   805	- **Admit build scripts under a sandbox, a warning, or an allowlist of
   806	  "well-known" crates.** Rejected: decision 0008 section 7 forbids answering a
   807	  package-selected execution surface with a runtime allowance, and an allowlist
   808	  of crate names is a trust store the protocol has no way to verify. The
   809	  measured fact that `cargo metadata` reports `custom-build` targets without
   810	  running them is what makes outright rejection implementable, so nothing is
   811	  gained by weakening it.
   812	- **Admit procedural macros because so much of the ecosystem depends on them.**
   813	  Rejected for the same reason, and with the cost acknowledged plainly: a
   814	  derive-heavy crate cannot be built by this driver. The honest consequence is a
   815	  narrower admitted dependency set, not a weaker execution boundary.
   816	- **Let the package's `.cargo/config.toml` configure the build, since it is the
   817	  ecosystem's normal configuration surface.** Rejected: it was **measured** to
   818	  execute a package-named script as a `rustc` wrapper, and its `[source]`,
   819	  `[registries]`, `[patch]` and `[http]` tables have no environment counterpart
   820	  and outrank the manager's own `$CARGO_HOME` config. It is refused outright,
   821	  and the environment neutralisation is kept as a second layer rather than as
   822	  the answer.
   823	- **Neutralise the config file with `--config` command-line arguments instead of
   824	  rejecting it.** Rejected: it would require the manager to enumerate every
   825	  dangerous key forever, and a key added by a future cargo would be admitted by
   826	  default. Rejecting the file is closed; enumerating overrides is open.
   827	- **Resolve the linker as `cc` like every other Rust build does, and fingerprint
   828	  the Apple toolchain that provides it.** Rejected on measurement: `cc` is a
   829	  `PATH` lookup and `xcrun` is a second one, both outside any fingerprinted
   830	  closure, and fingerprinting an Xcode toolchain to bring them inside would add
   831	  gigabytes of tree and an executable graph the manager does not control. Using
   832	  the `rust-lld` the distribution already ships removes both lookups and leaves
   833	  the SDK as data.
   834	- **Bind the platform SDK as a companion toolchain with its own identifier.**
   835	  Rejected: decision 0007's identifier set is closed at five, an SDK is not a
   836	  toolchain in that sense, and it contributes no process. Extending the
   837	  algorithm this decision owns to fingerprint an ordered closure of roots keeps
   838	  the closed set intact and keeps `toolchain_identities` at one element.
   839	- **Fingerprint only the SDK subtree the linker actually reads.** Rejected: the
   840	  linker is given `-syslibroot` and resolves within the SDK layout, so a subtree
   841	  is not a closure, and choosing one would make the fingerprint a guess about
   842	  linker behaviour that a toolchain update could invalidate silently. The
   843	  measured 9.01 s cost is stated instead.
   844	- **Memoise the toolchain fingerprint across operations to recover that cost.**
   845	  Rejected: decision 0007 says fingerprinting proves the tree is stable across an
   846	  operation and identical across operations, and a memo keyed on anything
   847	  cheaper than the content proves neither.
   848	- **Let `source_dir` name a package inside a Cargo workspace.** Rejected: the
   849	  member's own `Cargo.toml` would be a nearer ancestor than the build root's,
   850	  which contradicts decision 0008 section 4's nearest-ancestor rule, and reading
   851	  the rule loosely for Rust would make the one structural guarantee about
   852	  project metadata language-dependent. One package per build root is the cost.
   853	- **Add a `bin`, `package`, `features` or `profile` member to the build command
   854	  so a workspace could be addressed.** Rejected by decision 0008 section 4
   855	  directly: the consuming command key is the sole naming authority and any
   856	  further selector hands pipeline control back to untrusted data.
   857	- **Run the graph command with `--filter-platform` so the rejection matrix
   858	  matches what will actually be built.** Rejected: it makes the verdict
   859	  host-dependent, so the same snapshot could be admitted on one machine and
   860	  rejected on another, and the direction of the error is the wrong one — a
   861	  surface excluded on this host is still in the snapshot. The union graph
   862	  over-rejects deterministically, and that trade is stated in section 8 rather
   863	  than hidden.
   864	- **Reject Cargo features and `[profile]` tables along with everything else.**
   865	  Rejected: features select which package source compiles, which is the same
   866	  class of choice as a build constraint, and profiles cannot inject a flag on a
   867	  release toolchain once `-Z` and `cargo-features` are rejected. Rejecting them
   868	  would refuse ordinary packages while proving nothing.
   869	- **Treat `rust-toolchain.toml` as forbidden in its entirety because it was
   870	  measured to redirect the shim and to attempt a download.** Rejected: the same
   871	  file was measured to be completely inert against the direct resolution
   872	  decision 0007 mandates, so forbidding it would reject ordinary packages for a
   873	  property that only exists on a resolution path Curator never takes. `path`
   874	  stays `forbidden` because it names an origin; the rest is compared and
   875	  discarded.
   876	- **Keep `rust-version` as a single `compared` rule, as decision 0007's expected
   877	  table has it.** Rejected on measurement: the field has three host-independent
   878	  acceptance layers with distinct diagnostics, and a single comparison rule has
   879	  no outcome for a value the ecosystem itself cannot represent. Those values
   880	  would pass Stage B and fail later as a cargo error, after cache lookup — the
   881	  exact deferral a pre-compiler cross-check exists to remove.
   882	- **Measure Rust's representability layer with `cargo build`'s exit status.**
   883	  Rejected for the reason decision 0007 already established for Go: that status
   884	  is representability conjoined with the host gate and the edition floor, and it
   885	  scores every representable future release as unrepresentable. The isolated
   886	  measurement is `cargo metadata --no-deps`, which was **measured** to accept a
   887	  `rust-version` above the host with exit 0 and therefore structurally cannot be
   888	  applying the host gate.
   889	- **Recognise cargo's diagnostics by their leading `error:` line alone.**
   890	  Rejected: three distinct grammar rejections share the lead `error: expected a
   891	  version like "1.32"` and none of them names the value under test, so a
   892	  lead-only classifier answers for a family rather than an outcome — the same
   893	  defect decision 0007 retired on the Go side. The caret block's source line is
   894	  required as part of the recognised form because it is the only part that names
   895	  the value.
   896	- **Skip the `bin` target name check because it comes from the manager's own
   897	  graph phase.** Rejected: it comes from package bytes by way of the graph
   898	  phase, and it reaches an argument vector. It is validated against a closed
   899	  grammar before it is used, in the same spirit as every other manager-derived
   900	  value.
   901	- **Claim macOS amd64 because the standard library ships in the same root.**
   902	  Rejected: shipping a standard library is not evidence that the pipeline runs,
   903	  and decision 0008 section 9 requires immutable native evidence for the exact
   904	  tuple. The pair is a qualification obligation with a stated acceptance test.
   905	- **Ship a Windows path that resolves `link.exe` through a Visual Studio
   906	  activation script.** Rejected: it reintroduces exactly the `PATH`-resolved
   907	  executable that section 2 removes on macOS, and it would make the Windows
   908	  process graph depend on host state the manager neither selects nor
   909	  fingerprints. Windows waits for `lld-link` or self-contained evidence.
   910	- **Byte-scan package source for `include!` and reject on a match.** Rejected:
   911	  it is unsound in both directions — `include ! ( )` and `cfg_attr` forms escape
   912	  it, and the substring rejects ordinary comments — and an unsound check
   913	  presented as a guarantee is worse than a stated exposure. Section 13 states
   914	  it.
   915	
   916	## Compatibility impact
   917	
   918	This decision changes no bytes. It adds no schema, no vector, no generated case
   919	and no release metadata; it does not alter the rc.5 conformance manifest digest
   920	or any pin. Manifest schemas 1 through 7, `skill-build.json` schema 1, receipt
   921	schemas 1 and 2, marker schemas 1 through 3, claim schemas 1 through 3,
   922	`Skillfile.dev.json` schema 2, `manager-worker-v1`, `capability-evidence-v1`,
   923	`rc5-native-control-inventory-v1`, `curator-go-toolchain-v1`,
   924	`curator-build-source-v1` and every rc.4 and rc.5 conformance byte keep their
   925	exact contents and meanings, and every `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` identity is
   926	unchanged.
   927	
   928	`rust-v1` and `rust-repository-v1` remain reserved. Until `TASK-260728-251p01`
   929	moves them, every schema including manifest schema 8 as first minted MUST reject
   930	both, and a manager MUST treat each as an unknown driver.
   931	
   932	`curator-rust-toolchain-v1` is a new algorithm identifier that no existing
   933	artifact names, so introducing it moves no frozen byte. It does not reuse,
   934	extend or alias `curator-go-toolchain-v1`.
   935	
   936	The `rust-version` disposition changes from decision 0007's expected `compared`
   937	row to `classified`. That row was explicitly reserved for confirmation or
   938	correction by this decision, no schema or vector currently depends on it, and
   939	the change narrows what Curator accepts silently rather than widening it.
   940	
   941	This decision reserves `0009` as its decision number. Swift
   942	(`TASK-260728-1yhuqi`) and Kotlin (`TASK-260728-168smo`) are unstarted at the
   943	time of writing; if either lands `0009` first, this record renumbers rather than
   944	contests, exactly as `TASK-260728-2spy93` did when `0007` was claimed.
   945	
   946	## Security impact
   947	
   948	The central claim of section 2 is narrow, checkable and measured: on the probed
   949	host, neither the graph phase nor the compile phase resolves a single executable
   950	through `PATH`, and the control run without the linker and SDK pins resolves two.
   951	The whole process closure is three executables inside one fingerprinted
   952	distribution, which is a stronger property than the contract requires and
   953	strictly stronger than resolving a platform compiler driver would give.
   954	
   955	The exposure this driver adds relative to `go-v1` is real and is not minimised.
   956	A Rust build front end is a larger untrusted parser and code generator than the
   957	Go one, and Rust's mainstream build path executes package-selected code by
   958	design. Section 8 removes that path rather than containing it, and every removal
   959	is decided before the compile phase from data the graph phase was **measured**
   960	not to execute. What remains is the ordinary compiler-input exposure the
   961	portable policy already admits — denial of service through resource consumption,
   962	and compiler vulnerabilities reached by adversarial source — bounded by the
   963	parent-enforced deadline, output and artifact limits and by whichever
   964	native-control inventory entries the host provides, and by nothing stronger. The
   965	six deferred hardened guarantees are not claimed, named as controls, or implied.
   966	
   967	Two surfaces are admitted with bounds rather than rejected, and section 13 states
   968	both: compile-time file inclusion, which is a read the portable policy does not
   969	contain and which no existing deferred guarantee covers, and foreign function
   970	declarations against base-installation libraries, which the artifact class
   971	already admits. Neither is presented as closed.
   972	
   973	Fingerprinting remains honest about what it proves. It proves that both roots
   974	are stable across an operation and identical across operations. It does not
   975	prove that the Rust distribution is genuinely upstream's, and it does not prove
   976	that the SDK is genuinely Apple's; verifying either remains the operator's
   977	responsibility at configuration time, and this contract performs no signature
   978	verification of a toolchain or an SDK. A `tree_sha256` in a receipt must not be
   979	read as provenance.
   980	
   981	Refusing auto-install, prerelease hosts, package-selected paths, channels,
   982	mirrors, registries and version managers is inherited unchanged from decision
   983	0007 and is not reopened here. The one thing this decision adds to that surface
   984	is the link-support root, and it is resolved through the same two declaration
   985	channels, with the same forbidden origins and the same diagnostics, precisely so
   986	that admitting it introduces no new way for a package to influence what runs.

