   250	review. It MUST NOT be admitted by widening `native-executable-v1` or any of the
   251	eight driver identifiers.
   252	
   253	This version does not require bit-reproducible artifacts. Cache identity is
   254	keyed on the canonical build input, and the artifact digest records what a
   255	specific operation produced. A toolchain step that legitimately embeds
   256	non-reproducible bytes, such as a linker-applied ad-hoc signature, is compiler
   257	output and not a manager signing step, but it MUST be produced by the driver's
   258	fixed argument vector without selecting a signing identity, credential, or
   259	network interaction.
   260	
   261	### 4. Local source ownership: context-excluded build roots
   262	
   263	Local drivers reuse the schema-6 and schema-7 `build_roots` model without
   264	change. A local build root MUST be a portable relative path other than `.`, MUST
   265	name a real link-free directory in the immutable raw skill snapshot, MUST be
   266	unique and pairwise disjoint, MUST NOT equal, contain, or be contained by a
   267	runtime root, and MUST be referenced by at least one local build command. Build
   268	roots are statically excluded from agent context and from the runtime copy
   269	before cache lookup or any compiler discovery, and that exclusion applies
   270	identically to real builds, exact cache hits, and dry-runs.
   271	
   272	A schema-8 local build command, `buildCommandV8`, has exactly this
   273	package-controlled surface, with `driver` drawn from whichever local identifiers
   274	the admitted wire driver set holds when schema 8 is minted:
   275	
   276	```json
   277	{"type":"build","driver":"go-v1","source_dir":"build/cmd/tool",
   278	 "toolchain":{"id":"go","version":{"kind":"at_least","min":"1.23.0"}}}
   279	```
   280	
   281	The same shape carries a reserved identifier once its contract is accepted and
   282	it has moved into the admitted wire driver set — for instance
   283	`{"type":"build","driver":"rust-v1","source_dir":"build/cmd/tool",
   284	"toolchain":{"id":"rust","version":{"kind":"at_least","min":"1.82.0"}}}` — and
   285	until that move a manifest naming it is rejected as an unknown driver.
   286	
   287	The object MUST contain exactly `type`, `driver`, `source_dir`, and `toolchain`,
   288	all four REQUIRED, with `additionalProperties` false. `toolchain` is the closed
   289	`toolchain-requirement-v1` object owned by decision 0007: exactly `id` and
   290	`version`, where `id` MUST equal the driver's registry primary toolchain and
   291	`version` is exactly one of the three closed kinds. It is admitted as the fourth
   292	member for one reason and no other — it can only *filter* within the
   293	manager-trusted toolchain set. It cannot name, add, locate, download, activate,
   294	or switch a candidate, and it cannot reach the registry's tested-release
   295	`compatibility` set. Its shape, grammar, ordering, intersection, and diagnostics
   296	are decision 0007's, are not restated here, and MUST NOT be redefined by a
   297	driver contract.
   298	
   299	Placement is by reference to one shared definition and never by an inline
   300	object. In `schemas/v1/common.schema.json` the requirement object is the single
   301	definition `toolchainRequirementV1`, and in every slot that carries it the
   302	property schema MUST be exactly `{"$ref":"#/$defs/toolchainRequirementV1"}` with
   303	no sibling keyword. `toolchainRequirementV1` itself MUST be an object schema —
   304	`"type": "object"` exactly, never omitted and never unioned with another type —
   305	declaring exactly `id` and `version`, both REQUIRED, with `additionalProperties`
   306	false; decision 0007 owns everything inside `version`. The object type carries
   307	the closure rather than decorating it: `properties`, `required`, and
   308	`additionalProperties` constrain objects and say nothing about a string, so a
   309	definition that keeps the two members while admitting a scalar hands the package
   310	a free-text toolchain value through a slot the wire still calls closed. Naming
   311	the member is not carrying the object,
   312	so a slot declaring `toolchain` as a string, as an inline object, as an object
   313	carrying a path, root, URL, channel, mirror, or credential member, or as a
   314	reference to any other definition — in particular the resolved
   315	`goToolchainIdentityV1` toolchain identity, which is a build input rather than a
   316	gate — is not the requirement object and MUST be rejected. One definition, one
   317	reference form, and one closed member set is also what keeps the three slots
   318	from drifting apart once three driver contracts land independently.
   319	
   320	`buildCommandV6` is frozen at exactly `type`, `driver`, and `source_dir` and
   321	MUST NOT gain `toolchain` or any other member; a schema-6 or schema-7 command
   322	takes its driver's registry baseline requirement, exactly as decision 0007
   323	states.
   324	
   325	Beyond those four members no driver may add a package-controlled member. In
   326	particular a manifest MUST NOT express a binary, product, crate, module,
   327	target-name, feature, profile, configuration, optimization level, argv,
   328	environment, flag, tag, linker option, toolchain path, toolchain root, download
   329	location, mirror, channel or track, version-manager reference, install or
   330	package-manager command, output name or path, install destination, alias, PATH
   331	edit, signing identity, credential, trust root, hook, plugin, macro, generator,
   332	recipe, post-build action, fallback, or secondary artifact. A version constraint
   333	inside the closed `toolchain` object is the single admitted exception to the
   334	toolchain-related half of that list, and it is admitted only in the filtering
   335	sense above.
   336	
   337	Each local driver MUST bind exactly one closed driver-defined project-metadata
   338	file that MUST exist directly in the build root and MUST be the nearest ancestor
   339	of `source_dir`, exactly as `go-v1` binds `go.mod`. A manager MUST NOT discover,
   340	search for, or infer that file, the module, the target, the command, or the
   341	output. Every non-standard module, package, source, embedded, and vendored input
   342	selected by the driver's fixed graph phase MUST remain below the command's build
   343	root.
   344	
   345	Each local driver MUST define a deterministic, non-discovering mapping from
   346	`source_dir` to exactly one compiled program. A driver that cannot define such a
   347	mapping without a new package-controlled member MUST be rejected for this
   348	version. Widening the command object is not an option, because the consuming
   349	manifest command key is the sole executable name and the sole naming authority.
   350	
   351	### 5. External source ownership: `skill-build.json` schema 2
   352	
   353	The external envelope of decision 0005 is unchanged. The consuming manifest owns
   354	`build_repositories` and the command key; the repository owns only the
   355	descriptor; the manager owns acquisition, validation, audit, naming,
   356	publication, and every process.
   357	
   358	The schema-8 external build command, `repositoryBuildCommandV2`, MUST contain
   359	exactly `type`, `driver`, `repository`, `target`, and `toolchain`, all five
   360	REQUIRED, with `additionalProperties` false. `toolchain` is the same closed
   361	`toolchain-requirement-v1` object, admitted under exactly the reasoning of
   362	section 4. `repositoryBuildCommandV1` is frozen at its four members and MUST NOT
   363	gain a fifth.
   364	
   365	`skill-build.json` remains the sole descriptor filename, at the repository root
   366	and nowhere else, with no alias and no implementation-specific name. Schema 2
   367	changes exactly two things and nothing else, in the target object
   368	`skillBuildTargetV2`:
   369	
   370	1. `driver` becomes a `oneOf` over the admitted external identifiers; and
   371	2. one OPTIONAL member is added, `toolchain`, the same closed
   372	   `toolchain-requirement-v1` object, carried by exactly the same
   373	   `{"$ref":"#/$defs/toolchainRequirementV1"}` reference as the two schema-8
   374	   commands. OPTIONAL governs whether the member must be present, never what it
   375	   means: a present descriptor `toolchain` is the identical closed object, and
   376	   the malformed shapes rejected in section 4 are rejected here too.
   377	
   378	`driver`, `build_root`, and `source_dir` stay REQUIRED and keep their schema-1
   379	meaning, `additionalProperties` stays false, and the document still contains
   380	exactly `schema_version` and a non-empty `targets` map. `skillBuildTargetV1` is
   381	frozen at its three members and MUST NOT gain a fourth.
   382	
   383	The descriptor requirement is OPTIONAL rather than REQUIRED because the
   384	repository, not the consuming skill, is the party that knows what its own source
   385	needs, and a repository written before schema 2 must stay readable: an absent
   386	`toolchain` contributes no interval and the effective requirement is decided by
   387	the registry baseline intersected with the consuming manifest's REQUIRED
   388	requirement. Where both are present they intersect, which is associative and
   389	commutative, so no ownership question arises and an empty intersection is a
   390	deterministic host-independent rejection rather than a precedence rule.
   391	
   392	Beyond that OPTIONAL member, a descriptor MUST NOT express a build, install,
   393	test, or run command, argv, environment, feature, profile, configuration,
   394	product, target name, output name or path, toolchain path, toolchain root,
   395	channel or track, download location, mirror, version-manager reference, install
   396	or package-manager command, trust root, platform list, signing identity,
   397	credential, hook, plugin, macro, generator, recipe, post-build action, fallback,
   398	or secondary artifact. A repository declaring a version constraint can only
   399	narrow within the manager-trusted set; it can never introduce a candidate into
   400	it.
   401	
   402	The repository owns the descriptor schema version and the consuming manifest
   403	owns nothing about it. A manager that supports schema 8 MUST read descriptor
   404	schemas 1 and 2. Descriptor schema 1 remains frozen and can express only
   405	`go-repository-v1` targets. A `rust-repository-v1`, `swift-repository-v1`, or
   406	`kotlin-native-repository-v1` command therefore requires a schema-2 descriptor;
