   523	### 7.1 File-shape gate
   524	
   525	For each `metadata_sources` file present in the validated tree, the gate covers
   526	file **syntax** only: a file the ecosystem's own grammar rejects, including a
   527	key the ecosystem permits at most once appearing more than once, is
   528	`build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch` with `assertion` = `unclassifiable` and a
   529	`source_ref` naming the file or the field path. It asserts nothing about the
   530	semantics of fields the entry does not read.
   531	
   532	For `Cargo.toml` the gate is "the TOML document does not parse". A `rust-version`
   533	whose TOML *type* is wrong is deliberately **not** a gate case: the document
   534	parses and the field extracts as a TOML value, so it is classifier class 1 of
   535	section 7.2, for the same reason decision 0007 refused to route a shape-valid
   536	but unrepresentable Go value to its gate.
   537	
   538	### 7.2 Classifier — `Cargo.toml` `package.rust-version`
   539	
   540	Rust has **three host-independent acceptance layers** plus one host gate, where
   541	Go has two plus one. All four are measured, one value per fixture, with
   542	`cargo metadata --no-deps --format-version 1 --offline`:
   543	
   544	| Value | Exit | Layer | First diagnostic line |
   545	|---|---|---|---|
   546	| `"1.85"` | 0 | — | accepted, `rust_version` `1.85` |
   547	| `"1.85.0"` | 0 | — | accepted, `rust_version` `1.85.0` |
   548	| `1.85` | 101 | document | `error: invalid type: floating point '1.85', expected a semver or workspace` |
   549	| `"1.85.0-beta"` | 101 | grammar | `error: unexpected prerelease field, expected a version like "1.32"` |
   550	| `"not-a-version"` | 101 | grammar | `error: unexpected prerelease field, expected a version like "1.32"` |
   551	| `"1.85.0+build"` | 101 | grammar | `error: unexpected build field, expected a version like "1.32"` |
   552	| `"1.85.0.1"` | 101 | grammar | `error: expected a version like "1.32"` |
   553	| `"stable"` | 101 | grammar | `error: expected a version like "1.32"` |
   554	| `""` | 101 | grammar | `error: expected a version like "1.32"` |
   555	| `"1"` @ edition 2015 | 0 | — | accepted, `rust_version` `1` |
   556	| `"1"` @ edition 2018 | 101 | edition floor | `rust-version 1 is older than first version (1.31.0) required by the specified edition (2018)` |
   557	| `"1.31"` @ edition 2018 | 0 | — | accepted |
   558	| `"1.31"` @ edition 2021 | 101 | edition floor | `rust-version 1.31 is older than first version (1.56.0) required by the specified edition (2021)` |
   559	| `"1.56"` @ edition 2024 | 101 | edition floor | `rust-version 1.56 is older than first version (1.85.0) required by the specified edition (2024)` |
   560	| `"1.85"` @ edition 2024 | 0 | — | accepted |
   561	
   562	Measured edition floors: 2015 admits `"1"`; 2018 requires 1.31.0; 2021 requires
   563	1.56.0; 2024 requires 1.85.0. The floor depends on the manifest's own `edition`
   564	field and on no host input.
   565	
   566	The three layers are pairwise independent, and the independence is measured
   567	rather than argued: the document layer accepts `"not-a-version"` which the
   568	grammar layer rejects; the grammar layer accepts `"1"` which the edition floor
   569	rejects at edition 2018 and above; and the edition floor would accept `1.85`
   570	which the document layer rejects as a float. No layer contains another.
   571	
   572	**The host gate is excluded from the layer measurement.** **Measured**:
   573	`cargo metadata --no-deps` reports `rust_version` `1.99` on a 1.91.0 host with
   574	exit 0, so it structurally cannot be applying the host gate; and `cargo build
   575	--offline` with the same manifest exits 101 with
   576	`error: rustc 1.91.0 is not supported by the following package:` and
   577	`probe-future@0.1.0 requires rustc 1.99`, with compilation not started.
   578	
   579	Canonicalization: a value of `MAJOR` canonicalizes to `(MAJOR, 0, 0)`,
   580	`MAJOR.MINOR` to `(MAJOR, MINOR, 0)`, `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` to itself.
   581	
   582	The ordered exhaustive classifier. There is no `forbidden` class, because the
   583	field's value space is a version and nothing else. The catch-all is mandatory
   584	and last.
   585	
   586	| # | Class | Disposition | Outcome |
   587	|---|---|---|---|
   588	| 1 | the TOML value is not a string, or is the table form `{ workspace = true }` | — | `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch`, `assertion` `unclassifiable` |
   589	| 2 | a string the grammar cannot represent: a prerelease field, a build field, more than three dot-separated components, an empty string, or any non-numeric component | — | `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch`, `assertion` `unclassifiable` |
   590	| 3 | a grammar-representable string strictly below the floor of the manifest's `edition` | — | `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch`, `assertion` `unclassifiable` |
   591	| 4 | a grammar-representable string at or above the edition floor whose canonical triple is **above** the resolved toolchain triple | `compared` | `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch`, `assertion` the derived canonical `at_least` |
   592	| 5 | a grammar-representable string at or above the edition floor whose canonical triple is at or below the resolved toolchain triple | `compared` | permitted, and never honoured |
   593	| 6 | the field is absent | — | contributes no assertion |
   594	| 7 | anything else | — | `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch`, `assertion` `unclassifiable` |
   595	
   596	Classes 1 through 3 are host-independent, so their vectors need no Rust
   597	toolchain on the runner. Classes 4 and 5 take the resolved version as fixture
   598	input, exactly as decision 0007 fixes for every Stage B classification case.
   599	
   600	Class 1 includes the workspace-inheritance table because a workspace is rejected
   601	by G1 and the value would otherwise have no resolvable meaning. It is
   602	deliberately not routed to G1: a Stage B classification is a statement about a
   603	value, and G1 is a statement about the graph.
   604	
   605	### 7.3 Classifier — `rust-toolchain.toml` `toolchain.channel` and the legacy `rust-toolchain` file
   606	
   607	Decision 0007's channel table applies unchanged and is not restated: a
   608	canonicalizable version literal becomes an `at_least` assertion; `stable` is
   609	permitted and never honoured; `beta`, `nightly` and dated channels are a
   610	mismatch because they assert a prerelease host that is never resolved; anything
   611	else is `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch`, never a default and never a
   612	selector.
   613	
   614	The legacy one-line `rust-toolchain` file carries a bare channel string and uses
   615	the identical classifier. Its file-shape gate is "the file is not exactly one
   616	line of printable non-empty content after trimming a single trailing newline".
   617	
   618	`toolchain.path` is `forbidden` and is evaluated before every `compared` class,
   619	so a file carrying both `path` and `nightly` is deterministically
   620	`build_toolchain_package_influence_forbidden` with the `toolchain-root`
   621	`origin_class`.
   622	
   623	### 7.4 Recognised command outcomes are a closed set
   624	
   625	The corroborating command classifier is closed. A recognised outcome is one
   626	whole diagnostic line, matched **exactly** against a form predicted before the
   627	command ran from the value under test and the probe's own fixed constants. An
   628	outcome outside the set is **unknown**, yields no verdict and fails the probe. A
   629	lead with an unconstrained tail, and a substring found anywhere in the output,
   630	are families rather than outcomes and MUST NOT be recognised.
   631	
   632	Every grammar and document rejection renders a caret block whose third line is
   633	exactly `<N> | rust-version = <literal>`, where `<N>` is the fixture's fixed
   634	line number and `<literal>` is the value under test as written. The recognised
   635	form is therefore the **pair** of one predicted first line and one predicted
   636	source line, because three distinct grammar rejections share the first line
   637	`error: expected a version like "1.32"` and none of the first lines names the
   638	value.
   639	
   640	| Class | First line, exact | Second element, exact |
   641	|---|---|---|
   642	| document | `error: invalid type: <toml-type-phrase>, expected a semver or workspace` | `<N> \| rust-version = <literal>` |
   643	| grammar/prerelease | `error: unexpected prerelease field, expected a version like "1.32"` | `<N> \| rust-version = <literal>` |
   644	| grammar/build | `error: unexpected build field, expected a version like "1.32"` | `<N> \| rust-version = <literal>` |
   645	| grammar/other | `error: expected a version like "1.32"` | `<N> \| rust-version = <literal>` |
   646	| edition floor | `error: failed to parse manifest at \`<manifest-path>\`` | `  rust-version <literal-body> is older than first version (<floor>) required by the specified edition (<edition>)` |
   647	| host gate | `error: rustc <host-version> is not supported by the following package:` | `  <package>@<version> requires rustc <literal-body>` |
   648	| accepted | exit 0 | `packages[0].rust_version` equals `<literal-body>` |
   649	
   650	`"1.32"`, the edition floors `1.31.0`, `1.56.0` and `1.85.0`, and the manifest
   651	line number are probe fixed constants. If a later cargo release changes any of
   652	them the probe turns red rather than quietly changing what it measures, which is
   653	the correct direction for a check whose purpose is to notice upstream moving.
   654	
   655	The command forms are the narrowest that exercise the layer under test:
   656	`cargo metadata --no-deps --format-version 1 --offline` for the three layers,
   657	because it was measured not to apply the host gate; and `cargo build --offline`
   658	as the corroborating measurement, whose outcome is classified into `accepted`,
   659	`rejected-document`, `rejected-grammar`, `rejected-edition`, `host-gate` and
   660	`unknown`, never into pass and fail.
   661	
   662	### 7.5 Closure is measured, not asserted
   663	
   664	The probe carries a closure section that feeds the classifier outcomes
   665	deliberately outside the recognised set and requires each to yield no verdict,
   666	reporting for every fabrication which of the two laundering directions it
   667	belongs to:
   668	
   669	- **direction A, acceptance laundering**: real unrelated command failures — a
   670	  missing manifest, an unreadable working directory, an unknown subcommand, a
   671	  `--locked` lock-file failure — must not be scored as upstream acceptance;
   672	- **direction B, rejection laundering**: every measured outcome cross-fed under
   673	  a different value, and every measured diagnostic extended the way a later
   674	  release would extend it, must not be scored as a rejection verdict for the
   675	  value actually under test.
   676	
   677	The extended-diagnostic checks are constructed and are disclosed as such: an
   678	outcome upstream has not yet written cannot be measured on any host. Taking text
   679	upstream did emit and changing it the way a later release would is the honest
   680	form of that check.
   681	
   682	### 7.6 Controls required to fail
   683	
   684	Each is runnable from the probe binary and each MUST fail; a control that stops
   685	failing is a regression.
   686	
   687	| # | Control | What it guards |
   688	|---|---|---|
   689	| C1 | an open classifier with a fall-through verdict | closure in both directions |
   690	| C2 | lead-only recognition, dropping the caret source line | three grammar classes collapsing into one |
   691	| C3 | exit status as the semantic measurement (`cargo build` exit 0 means representable) | folding the host gate and the edition floor into the grammar layer |
   692	| C4 | `cargo build` as the isolated representability command | the same folding, arrived at by choosing a wider command |
   693	| C5 | the edition floor folded into the grammar classifier | one host-independent layer swallowing another |
   694	| C6 | substring matching anywhere in combined output | recognising a family rather than an outcome |
   695	
   696	C3 and C4 are not redundant: C3 changes how the outcome is read, C4 changes
   697	which command produces it, and either alone leaves the other's defect
   698	reachable.
