   364	graph is untouched. Only the phase they sit in changes.
   365	
   366	### 5. Typed diagnostics and manager-owned guidance
   367	
   368	Twelve stable `build_toolchain_*` codes cover requirement validity,
   369	unsatisfiable intersection, availability, undetermined version, incompatible
   370	version, untested release family, prerelease host, untrusted resolution,
   371	unsupported platform, metadata mismatch, package influence, and mid-operation
   372	change. They are listed with their exact trigger and stage in the reference
   373	document. Where two gates would both reject a host, the Stage A step order
   374	decides which code is reported, so the outcome is deterministic.
   375	
   376	Every code fires only at named steps of named stages, and the triggers that read
   377	as overlapping are partitioned by input rather than merely ordered. On the wire
   378	surface a malformed value is always `requirement_invalid`, so
   379	`package_influence_forbidden` is a Stage B code only. Within Stage A, resolution
   380	splits into declaration presence, origin admissibility, and shape, over three
   381	disjoint inputs: the channel map, the channel entry, and the filesystem object
   382	that entry names. `unavailable` means *nothing was declared* and is the only
   383	code the presence sub-step produces — a toolchain installed and on `PATH` but
   384	absent from both channels is `unavailable`, not `untrusted`, because `PATH` is
   385	not a channel. `untrusted` means *something was declared and is unusable*, which
   386	includes a declared root that does not exist and a missing primary executable.
   387	The partition is declaration presence, not severity, so the reported code never
   388	depends on how far the manager got before failing — and it matches the guidance
   389	classes, `host` for "obtain a toolchain" and `configuration` for "fix the
   390	configuration".
   391	
   392	Four codes fire at more than one site, each for a structural reason rather than
   393	an editorial one: `requirement_unsatisfiable` and `incompatible` have one site
   394	per interval they can be evaluated against, `platform_unsupported` one per half
   395	of platform applicability, and `metadata_mismatch` one for file shape and one
   396	for value classification.
   397	
   398	The diagnostic payload is therefore a discriminated union keyed on the **firing
   399	site** — `(code, stage, discriminant)` — and its shape stays derived rather than
   400	chosen: a payload carries exactly the values established at the site where it
   401	fires, and because every stage's steps are totally ordered, that set is a
   402	function of the site. A code declares at most one discriminant, a REQUIRED
   403	closed-token member; `untrusted` declares `substep` and `platform_unsupported`
   404	declares `check`. Keying on the code alone stopped working once one code could
   405	fire in two stages with different values established, and it also removed the
   406	last editorial choice in the union: `platform_unsupported` previously carried
   407	"either the supported set or the target pair" with nothing saying which, and
   408	`check` now selects that branch from the site. `effective_requirement` is absent
   409	at exactly the three sites whose own interval computation produced none, and
   410	`resolved_version` at exactly the seven that precede normalization. The two
   411	codes that motivated the union are representable without sentinels:
   412	`requirement_invalid` carries a location and a closed violation token, and
   413	`requirement_unsatisfiable` carries the individually validated fragments plus
   414	the two bounds whose ordering failed, naming every source achieving each bound
   415	in a fixed order so the payload is as source-order-independent as the
   416	intersection. A payload never reproduces an unvalidated package byte, which
   417	keeps package-controlled text out of manager output and keeps the payload
   418	bounded.
   419	
   420	A diagnostic carries a `guidance_id`, never prose or a URL. The
   421	`toolchain-guidance-catalog-v1` catalog is manager-owned, versioned, and total
   422	over (toolchain, reason, supported platform) — where `reason` is exactly the
   423	diagnostic code's suffix, so the code-to-reason mapping is the identity and
   424	stays total as codes are added. There is therefore no runtime "guidance
   425	missing" case for any of the twelve, including the authoring and configuration
   426	ones. Each reason declares a `guidance_class` that fixes the admissible origin
   427	of its `primary_source`: the language's own official origin for host reasons,
   428	the manager's operator documentation for configuration reasons, and this
   429	specification for authoring reasons. Every one of those is a manager-trusted
   430	origin, and guidance is text plus URL only — never a package, a mirror, an
   431	installer script, or a command the manager runs.
   432	
   433	Identifiers carry an immutable revision, `toolchain.<id>.<reason>.<platform>.r<N>`,
   434	because a bare tuple cannot name two entries and `superseded_by` would have
   435	nothing to point at. At most one entry per tuple is active; supersession moves
   436	to the next revision of the same tuple and retired entries are retained so an
   437	older diagnostic stays resolvable. Because the catalog is presentation, it is
   438	deliberately not a cache, receipt, marker, or claim input: a corrected URL or a
   439	new revision must never invalidate an artifact.
   440	
   441	Coverage is defined by the selection function rather than beside it, so
   442	"one `any` entry" and "one entry per operating system" stop competing: an active
   443	exact entry covers its operating system, an active `any` entry covers the rest,
   444	and pure-`any`, pure-per-OS, and hybrid catalogs are all valid. The gate checks
   445	two properties — every supported operating system resolves to exactly one active
   446	entry, and every active entry is reachable by some request — which admits a
   447	fallback plus overrides while rejecting dead entries and a fallback shadowed
   448	everywhere. Append-only is likewise stated against the version rather than
   449	against the entry: a published catalog version is immutable in whole, changes
   450	happen only from version N to N+1, and across that boundary the entry set only
   451	grows while `active` and `superseded_by` move one way, `true` to `false` and
   452	absent to set. Retirement without a successor is admissible only for a tuple the
   453	coverage gate no longer requires.
   454	
   455	**v1 never auto-installs.** No download, install, update, activation, or switch
   456	of a toolchain, and specifically not through `rustup`, `swiftly`, `sdkman`,
   457	`asdf`, `mise`, Homebrew, `winget`, the Gradle wrapper, or `GOTOOLCHAIN`.
   458	
   459	### 6. Identity effects
   460	
   461	The resolved toolchain identity stays a build input, as today; new drivers
   462	bind an ordered `toolchain_identities` array covering primary and companions,
   463	while `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` keep their existing single-field shape
   464	byte-for-byte.
   465	
   466	The effective requirement and the `compatibility` set are **gates, not build
   467	inputs**. Two packages whose different constraints are both satisfied by the
   468	same resolved toolchain produce the same artifact, so putting either in the key
   469	would fragment cache identity while adding no guarantee — the same argument
   470	that kept host capability evidence out of the key in decision 0006. There is no
   471	bypass: Stage A gates before cache lookup, so a hit is only reachable when the
   472	currently resolved toolchain passes both gates.
   473	
   474	That ordering also settles a case that is easy to state wrongly. If the current
   475	resolved toolchain is incompatible, the operation fails at Stage A — cache
   476	lookup is never reached, no candidate is consulted, and nothing is rebuilt.
   477	Rebuilding is the outcome of a *different* case: the current toolchain passes
   478	both gates and the cached candidate carries a different resolved toolchain
   479	identity, so the key does not match and the command rebuilds. There is no
   480	"cache hit with an incompatible toolchain" path.
   481	
   482	Currentness is unchanged and stays a property of recorded identity. A toolchain
   483	upgrade already changes the fingerprint, the cache key, and therefore
   484	currentness. Read-only status and audit report a Stage A or Stage B failure as
   485	a finding and MUST NOT mark an otherwise valid marker non-current because of
   486	it; install, upgrade, repair, and coverage-claiming audit fail with the typed
   487	diagnostic before mutation.
   488	
   489	## Rejected alternatives
   490	
   491	- **Per-driver toolchain rules.** Rejected: four drivers would produce four
   492	  version formats, four resolution stories, and four independent chances to
   493	  admit a package-selected path. The shared contract is the only place the
   494	  exclusion list can be exhaustive.
   495	- **Honor `rust-toolchain.toml`, `GOTOOLCHAIN`, `.swift-version`, or the Gradle
   496	  wrapper as selectors, since they are the ecosystem norm.** Rejected: each is
   497	  a direct package-bytes-to-executable-selection path, which is the boundary
   498	  decisions 0004 and 0006 exist to hold. They are retained as assertions
   499	  because cross-checking them produces a better diagnostic than a compiler
   500	  error, and discarded as selectors.
   501	- **Auto-install a missing toolchain.** Rejected: it makes the manager download
   502	  and execute third-party installer code outside every gate in this protocol,
   503	  and it converts a clean fail-fast into the largest untrusted action in the
   504	  operation. Guidance IDs to primary sources give the user the same outcome
   505	  with the decision left where it belongs.
   506	- **Free-form version constraint strings** (`">=1.23, <1.25"`). Rejected: a
   507	  mini-language invites two conforming managers to parse the same package
   508	  differently. A closed object with three kinds has one reading.
   509	- **Native version strings as the comparison domain.** Rejected: `go1.23.4`,
   510	  `1.79.0-nightly`, `5.10`, and `2.1.0-Beta1` have no shared order. Normalizing
   511	  to a triple at the entry boundary moves all per-language messiness into one
   512	  reviewed rule per toolchain.
   513	- **Rank prereleases instead of rejecting them.** Rejected: it requires a
   514	  cross-language prerelease order that does not exist, and it binds an unstable
   515	  compiler identity into a cache key that promises reproducibility.
   516	- **Let the version requirement alone decide admissibility, with no tested-family
   517	  set.** Rejected: it silently weakens the released rule. `profiles/manager.md`
   518	  section 2.2 requires rejecting an otherwise unknown release including one
   519	  merely ordered after a known one, and under a bare `at_least 1.23.0` an
   520	  untested Go 1.99.0 would be admitted. Ordering is not evidence of testing, so
   521	  the manager-owned set is the only thing that can carry that rule.
   522	- **Let a package narrow or widen the tested-family set, or express it as a
   523	  requirement.** Rejected: it is the same package-selects-the-compiler path in a
   524	  different spelling. The requirement filters within what the manager already
   525	  trusts; nothing on the wire surface may reach the trusted set itself.
   526	- **Treat every channel-valued field as forbidden package influence.** Rejected:
   527	  it conflates the surface Curator owns with the surface the ecosystem owns. On
   528	  the manifest and descriptor there is no channel field to forbid, so the rule
   529	  is vacuous there; in source metadata a channel is the ecosystem's normal way
   530	  of stating an expectation, and refusing to honor it while comparing it gives a
   531	  better diagnostic than a compiler error at no cost. Forbidding it outright
   532	  would reject ordinary packages and prove nothing.
   533	- **Classify a wire value that looks like a path, URL, or track as package
   534	  influence rather than as a malformed literal.** Rejected: it makes the code
   535	  depend on inferring intent from a byte string, and two conforming managers
   536	  would draw the line differently — is `1.23-corp` a bad literal or a smuggled
   537	  distribution name? The closed canonical grammar answers without inference, and
   538	  the field-addition half of the rule moves to a schema release gate, where it
   539	  is actually checkable. Keeping a runtime code for a field that a closed schema
   540	  cannot admit would have been dead text.
   541	- **Order the Stage A availability and trust triggers by severity instead of
   542	  partitioning them.** Rejected: severity ordering still leaves both descriptions
   543	  true for a declared-but-broken root, so the reported code would depend on how
   544	  far the manager happened to get before failing. Partitioning on declaration
   545	  presence gives each code one producing sub-step and matches the two remedies
   546	  the guidance classes already distinguish.
   547	- **Route a declared root that does not exist to `unavailable`.** Rejected: it
   548	  reads naturally — nothing is there — but it splits one operator mistake across
   549	  two codes depending on whether the missing thing is the root or the executable
   550	  inside it, and it sends "your configuration points at nothing" to `host`
   551	  guidance that says "install a toolchain".
   552	- **Treat a custom Go toolchain name such as `go1.23.4-bigcorp` as a metadata
   553	  mismatch.** Rejected: a mismatch says "the assertion is false about the
